 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "Call order when a view raises:\n\n1. `run_full_request` (`src/tessel/app.py:~1017`) catches the exception from `run_view` and calls `route_user_fault(ctx, e)`.\n2. `route_user_fault` (`src/tessel/app.py:865`) branches:\n   - If `e` is an `HTTPException` and `trap_http_exception` (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:894`) returns False, it calls `route_http_fault` (`src/tessel/app.py:830`). That method calls `_pick_fault_handler` and returns `e` itself if no handler is found.\n   - Otherwise it calls `_pick_fault_handler` directly. If that finds no handler, it re-raises the exception.\n3. `_pick_fault_handler` (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:869`) does the selection. It calls `_get_exc_class_and_code` (`src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:665`) to get the exception class and any HTTP code. It then looks in `error_handler_spec[bundle_name_or_None][code_or_None]`. That dict is filled by `fault_handler` / `register_error_handler` (`src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:606` / `:650`). The lookup order is:\n   1. Bundle handler for the specific code.\n   2. App handler for the specific code.\n   3. Bundle handler for the exception class.\n   4. App handler for the exception class.\n\n   Within each map, it walks the exception's MRO, so the most specific class wins.\n4. The chosen handler runs through `ensure_sync(handler)(e)`.\n5. If nothing handles the exception, or the handler itself raises, `wsgi_entry` (`src/tessel/app.py:~1566-1600`) calls `route_unhandled_fault` (`src/tessel/app.py:897`). That method wraps the exception in an `InternalServerError`. It then calls `_pick_fault_handler` again for a 500 handler, unless `PROPAGATE_EXCEPTIONS` is set, in which case it re-raises.",
